160
00:06:12,319 --> 00:06:16,639
The magnetic dipole moment is defined as

161
00:06:14,639 --> 00:06:19,520
the current through the wire multiplied

162
00:06:16,639 --> 00:06:21,120
by the cross-sectional area it encloses

163
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:22,960
multiplied by a unit vector

164
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:24,319
perpendicular to that surface. So the

165
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:26,880
dipole moment points in the

166
00:06:24,319 --> 00:06:29,680
perpendicular direction.

167
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:32,240
Similarly, a proton spins on its axis at

168
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:34,400
the subatomic level creating a magnetic

169
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:37,919
dipole moment that can be thought of as

170
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:40,000
many tiny circulating currents.

171
00:06:37,919 --> 00:06:42,400
When placed in an external magnetic

172
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,199
field, this dipole experiences a torque

173
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:46,960
expressed as toao equals mu crossb,

174
00:06:45,199 --> 00:06:49,360
which makes it tend to align with the

175
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:51,680
field. However, because of the proton's

176
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:53,840
angular momentum, it cannot align

177
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:55,759
completely. Instead, it underos

178
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:58,560
procession wobbling around the field

179
00:06:55,759 --> 00:07:00,240
axis like a spinning top.

180
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:03,280
So, think of the proton as a spinning

181
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:05,520
top. It rotates on its axis but also

182
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:08,639
processes about an axis that is parallel

183
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:11,280
to the external magnetic field.

184
00:07:08,639 --> 00:07:13,599
Now let's introduce radiation. We emit

185
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:16,080
an electromagnetic wave laterally toward

186
00:07:13,599 --> 00:07:18,000
the procession axis.

187
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:20,160
For simplicity, I've drawn only the

188
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,599
magnetic component oscillating in and

189
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:24,160
out of the board as it travels

190
00:07:21,599 --> 00:07:25,919
laterally. If the angular frequency of

191
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:28,000
this radiation equals the angular

192
00:07:25,919 --> 00:07:29,759
frequency of procession, resonance

193
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,479
occurs.

194
00:07:29,759 --> 00:07:36,000
We define the lore angular frequency.

195
00:07:32,479 --> 00:07:38,800
Omega L equals gamma* B.

196
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:43,039
Here gamma is the gyromagnetic ratio.

197
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:45,759
For a proton, gamma is about 2.67 * 10 8

198
00:07:43,039 --> 00:07:48,960
radians/s per Tesla. To convert from

199
00:07:45,759 --> 00:07:54,879
radians/s to hertz, we divide by 2 pi.

200
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:58,240
So 2 p= gamma * b or equivalently l=

201
00:07:54,879 --> 00:08:00,319
gamma over 2 pi * b.

202
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:02,800
Numerically, this works out to about 42

203
00:08:00,319 --> 00:08:04,960
meghertz per Tesla.

204
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:07,120
The applied radiation is tuned to the

205
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:09,680
proton's precession frequency, so the

206
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:12,720
magnetic field interacts each cycle,

207
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:14,720
gradually flattening the motion.

208
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:16,560
When the radiation stops, the proton

209
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:19,120
returns to precessing around the

210
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:21,039
external field. This is the basis of

211
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:23,280
